# Guardrails

- For any new feature or non-trivial change: propose the interface/plan
  first. Wait for explicit approval before writing the implementation.
- Before any multi-file change: create a git checkpoint
  (git commit -m "checkpoint: before <task>").
- Never assume a package, symbol, or API exists from training familiarity
  alone - verify it against the actual codebase or package manifest first.
- Keep changes scoped to what was asked. If a broader refactor seems
  warranted, propose it separately rather than bundling it in.
- When explaining a change, state the WHY (the problem and the tradeoff),
  not just a restatement of the diff.
- Respect existing architectural boundaries (e.g. ports & adapters layering)
  without being told each time. If a request seems to require crossing one
  (e.g. having the domain core depend on a GUI or driven-adapter type),
  stop and ask instead of silently doing it.
